{"type":"assistant","message":{"type":"message","model":"kimi-k3","usage":{"input_tokens":56568,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_5_m_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_1_h_tokens":0},"role":"assistant","id":"02036ae7d1ce0a7aed1f798128a91fbd","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait — 4:14 and 4:21 look ORIGINAL and INTACT! 6 texts each with the original content, original fills (light gray #F7F7FA-ish), positioned correctly (0,0 and 668,0). They were NOT modified!\n\nSo what happened earlier? The walk showed weird content because... the FIRST failed call (before the crash): `const home = F('01_Home')` created a frame, and I appended it to... `page.appendChild(home)` where page = currentPage = 0:1. That appended my new empty frame to Start Here. Then the crash.\n\nBut the walk I did on \"01_Home\" found texts \"Goal: ...\" etc. That was `figma.currentPage.findOne(n => n.name === '01_Home')` — found 4:14 (the brief card) because my new empty frame... wait, where did my new frame go? findOne returns first match in tree order. If my new frame was appended at the END of page 0:1, findOne would find 4:14 first (earlier in tree). Yes! So walk showed 4:14 — everything I saw was the UNTOUCHED original. \n\nAnd in my second build call, `page.findOne(n => n.name === '01_Home')`... wait no, in the second call I created `home` fresh via F() and appendChild. The crash at line 91 happened during... the second call crashed at `heroBtn.findOne(...)` — no wait, that was the FIRST call. The second call crashed at line 91 which is also findOne TEXT characters. Let me check: did the second call actually create a new home frame in workspace? It appended `home` to `page` (5:2)... then crashed at heroBtn.findOne. But the hero button crash — Button/Primary/Icon component (7:12) has a TEXT child \"Button\". findOne on the instance should find it.\n\nHmm wait, actually the second crash line 91: `heroBtn.findOne(n => n.type === 'TEXT').characters = 'See all listings'`. Unless... `btnIconComp.createInstance()` — btnIconComp came from compByName('Button / Primary / Icon'). That looks up lib.findOne... — the library is on page 5:2 and currentPage is 5:2, fine. The instance should contain the text.\n\nUnless findOne with predicate doesn't traverse INTO instance children in this API version? For INSTANCES, `findOne` requires the callback... no, findAll works with predicates on instances (used earlier successfully: `wBtn.findOne(n => n.type === 'TEXT').characters = 'Work with us'` — that was in the Header creation call which SUCCEEDED).\n\nHmm wait, did it? The header creation succeeded and returned IDs. In that call I used `wBtn.findOne(n => n.type === 'TEXT').characters = 'Work with us'` — worked.\n\nSo why did heroBtn.findOne fail?? Difference: in header call, btnIconComp was obtained via comp() helper — same thing.\n\nOK wait, maybe the crash was NOT heroBtn. Line 91 in the second call... Let me count: the code had `hLeft.appendChild(heroBtn)` near line 80ish, then hPhotoWrap stuff, listings section... line 91 could be `seeAll.findOne(n => n.type === 'TEXT').characters = 'See all listings'`. The Link/Arrow component (7:42) has TEXT \"Learn more\". Instance findOne TEXT should work...\n\nOR maybe the crash was in the home-cards loop: `card.findOne(n => n.type === 'TEXT' && n.fontSize === 20)`. fontSize on a TEXT node is fine. Hmm, but if findOne returns null → crash. Inside an INSTANCE, does `n.fontSize` throw for non-text nodes? No — predicate short-circuits: n.type === 'TEXT' first, && short-circuits, so fontSize only accessed on texts.\n\nWait, actually — could `findOne` on an instance with a predicate that accesses `.type` fail on... no.\n\nLet me check what state the workspace is in now: does a new \"01_Home\" frame exist on 5:2 with partial content (header instance + hero left texts)? The walk earlier showed home.children[1] = TEXT \"Goal:...\" — that was actually 4:14's child. So the question stands. Let me list workspace children.\n\nAlso importantly: my FIRST call created `home` frame and appended to page 0:1! Did it? Yes: `page.appendChild(home)` where page = 0:1. That frame was named '01_Home' too, empty at crash time (crash happened before hero append? The crash was at heroBtn line... wait first call crashed at line 105 which is the filter loop `f.findOne(n => n.name === 'Label')`. So by then, home had: header instance, hero (with content texts, button), listings (heading, link)... all appended to frame 4:14?? NO — appended to `home`, my new frame. My new frame was a child of page 0:1.\n\nThen when I moved \"4:14\" back... I moved the WRONG frame? No — I moved node id 4:14 explicitly by ID. My new frame had a different ID (7:x). So 4:14 went back correctly.\n\nBut my partial home frame (7:x named '01_Home') remained a child of page 0:1! Then in the second call, `page.findOne(n => n.name === '01_Home')` — page=5:2, finds nothing, creates fresh. Wait no, in second call I didn't findOne first — I just created. Hmm, but the SECOND call crashed at line 91 — before creating cards. And its partial frame went to 5:2 correctly.\n\nThen my inspection call `figma.currentPage.findOne(n => n.name === '01_Home')` with currentPage=0:1 found 4:14 (tree order: brief card comes before my appended partial frame at end of page). So I misread everything. The partial 7:x frame still sits on page 0:1 — I need to find and DELETE it from page 0:1!\n\nAnd the second partial home frame on 5:2 — from the crashed second call — contains header instance + hero + listings-heading + filter? It crashed at line 91... let me figure out which line that was. The error was `cannot set property 'characters' of null`. In the second call, candidate findOne calls: heroBtn (line ~80), seeAll (~line 100?), filter Label/Placeholder (~110), searchBtn (~118), card texts (~135+). Line 91 → likely heroBtn.findOne... Hmm.\n\nWhy would heroBtn.findOne(n => n.type === 'TEXT') return null? OH WAIT. I think I finally get it: `createInstance()` on a component that's on the SAME page is fine... but the crash earlier in call 1 was `f.findOne(n => n.name === 'Label')` on a Field/Filter instance — the instance's label text IS named \"Label\"... in the clone. Hmm, unless instance children get named differently.\n\nHold on, let me reconsider: in call 1, `filterComp.createInstance()` where filterComp = comp('Field / Filter')... but wait, in call 1 I never reached the filter loop because... no, call 1 DID reach it — crash line 105 = filter loop. Call 1 successfully created heroBtn with `.characters = 'See all listings'` at line ~91! So findOne TEXT on Button/Icon instance WORKED in call 1.\n\nSo in call 1 the failure was `f.findOne(n => n.name === 'Label')` — Field/Filter instance has no child named \"Label\"?! The component Field/Filter (9:35) — when I cloned givenTF and renamed, clone children kept names \"Label\"/\"Input\"/\"Placeholder\"... but wait, I renamed the COMPONENT to 'Field / Filter'. Instance children: when you instantiate, child names come from component children. The component 9:35 dump earlier showed children: TEXT \"Looking for\", FRAME \"Input\", TEXT \"Any\"?? NO — the earlier dump showed:\n\n```\nCOMPONENT Field / Filter\n  TEXT Looking for\n  FRAME Input\n    TEXT Any\n    INSTANCE Icon_Chevron_Down\n```\n\nThe dump prints node.name — the TEXT child is named \"Looking for\" NOT \"Label\"! Why? Because when I set `f.findOne(n => n.name === 'Label').characters = 'Looking for'` in the library-building call... no wait, that call renamed characters but node names... TEXT nodes in Figma: when you create a text node, its NAME auto-syncs to its characters (when name is default/auto-generated). The given Text Field component's label was named \"Label\" because... hmm, the original 1:26 was named \"Label\" with characters \"Label\" — auto-named. When I changed characters to 'Looking for', the name auto-updated to 'Looking for' (since it was auto-generated). YES! That's it. The dump of Field/Filter internals confirmed: \"TEXT Looking for\", \"TEXT Any\".\n\nSo in the library clones, text node names follow their CURRENT characters, not \"Label\"/\"Placeholder\". That's why findOne(name==='Label') → null → crash. And the \"Placeholder\" findOne in filter loop would also fail (it's named \"Any\").\n\nAnd in call 2, the crash at line 91: hmm, heroBtn.findOne(n => n.type === 'TEXT') — the Button component's text child — in Button/Primary/Icon (7:12) I set characters to 'Button'... its name likely auto-updated to 'Button'. findOne by TYPE should still find it regardless of name. So line 91 ≠ heroBtn? Let me recount call 2's lines... The seeAll link: `seeAll.findOne(n => n.type === 'TEXT')` — Link/Arrow text named 'Learn more', find by type OK. Hmm.\n\nActually wait — maybe the issue is different: does `findOne` with a predicate traverse instance INTERNALS? In header creation I used `wBtn.findOne(n => n.type === 'TEXT')` successfully... that returned something since no crash. OK so type-predicates work on instances.\n\nHmm, so what was call 2 line 91? Let me count the actual code lines of call 2... Lines 1-10: page/lib/fonts/C. 11-30: helpers. 31-35: compById etc. ~37: home frame creation... hero ~48-70. heroBtn ~72-75: `const heroBtn = btnIconComp.createInstance(); heroBtn.findOne(n => n.type === 'TEXT').characters = 'See all listings';` — could be around line 76-80. Then photo ~82-88. listings ~90+: `const seeAll = ...; seeAll.findOne(...)`. Line 91 might be `heroPhoto.resize` no...\n\nHonestly rather than count, the productive path: the instance child naming insight explains filter crashes. For buttons/links I use type-predicates which work. For Field/Filter instances I'll target texts by CURRENT name or by type+position: e.g. findOne(n => n.type === 'TEXT' && n.parent.name !== 'Input') hmm. Better: use indices — instance.children[0] is the label text (top-level), and within Input frame, children[0] is placeholder text. That's robust: `f.children[0]` (label), `f.findOne(n => n.name === 'Input').children[0]` (placeholder). Wait — does direct children access work on instances? Yes, instance.children is accessible.\n\nNow cleanup tasks:\n1. Find stray partial frames on page 0:1 named '01_Home' (id 7:x) — DELETE.\n2. Check page 5:2 children: partial '01_Home' from call 2 — inspect what it has; either continue from it or delete and rebuild. Simpler: delete and rebuild fresh with corrected code (I have all the code; fixing findOne calls).\n3. Verify 4:14/4:21 truly pristine (they look fine — text contents match original metadata exactly, fills original light gray, positions original).\n\nActually wait — one more check on 4:14: in the very first failed call, `home.appendChild(headerComp.createInstance())` etc. — no. In first call, `home` = my new frame; nothing was appended to 4:14. In second call same. So 4:14/4:21 never received children. The 6 texts match the original dump (4:15,4:16,4:17,4:18,4:19,4:20 → names \"01_Home\", \"Goal:...\", \"MUST INCLUDE\", \"•...\", \"LINKS\", \"→ See all listings...\"). Sizes match original metadata (82x26, 596x20, 84x16, 596x360, 33x16, 596x20). 4:21 matches too (110x26, 596x360, 596x40 last text). Positions 0,0 and 668,0 match. Fills light gray — original card color. They're pristine. \n\nBut wait — 4:14 clipsContent=true. Original? Probably was already (it's a card). Fine.\n\nAlso the first call appended texts?? No... first call created T() nodes — created on currentPage (0:1) at 0,0! figma.createText() adds to current page root. In call 1, many T() nodes were created before being appended into hLeft etc. — all got appended. But in call 1, crash happened in filter loop AFTER `T('Find your next place to live', ...)` was appended. Before crash, did any T() get created but not appended? The filter loop crashed at findOne BEFORE creating T nodes inside loop (no T creation there). searchBtn... not reached. So no orphan texts on 0:1 from call 1? Actually — `eyebrow()` T created then appended. h1, hCopy appended. All good. BUT: the instances created via createInstance() are also added to current page root before being appended — all were appended. OK.\n\nHowever — in call 1, `home` frame (7:x, '01_Home') was appended to page 0:1 root with all the hero/listings content inside. That's the stray to delete. Its content includes instances of my components — deleting it doesn't affect components.\n\nAlso `lib` — call 1 created lib on page 0:1?? NO WAIT. lib was created in Part 1 which ran with currentPage=5:2 (I set it at the start of Part 1). Then calls 2/3 (Part 2 + header/footer) also had currentPage=5:2? Part 2 didn't set current page — it used `figma.currentPage` — which was still 5:2 from Part 1. Header/footer call also didn't set page — appended header/footer components to `lib` explicitly — fine regardless of page.\n\nThe 01_Home build call 1: `const page = figma.currentPage;` — hmm wait, actually at that point currentPage was still 5:2?? But the inspection showed currentPage = '0:1'! When did it change? AH — I see. In call 1 of the home build I wrote `const page = figma.currentPage` — if that was 5:2, home would've gone to 5:2 and no corruption... but currentPage was 0:1 per inspection. Who switched it? Possibly the MCP server or the user opened the file at Start Here page in Figma desktop between calls, changing currentPage. That's plausible — the file may be open in someone's Figma client following along (benchmark harness). That also explains: between my calls, \"someone\" (the viewer) was on page 0:1.\n\nAnyway: in home-build call 1, `figma.createText()`/frames created on currentPage root = 0:1 root, then appended into `home` which was appended to page(0:1) root. Consistent.\n\nIn home-build call 2 (after my setCurrentPageAsync(5:2)): currentPage=5:2, so everything went to 5:2 correctly. It crashed at line 91 — on what? Since texts T() in call 2... Let me think about which findOne could return null there. Candidates by type predicate — those work. By name: NONE in call 2 until the filter loop: `f.findOne(n => n.name === 'Label')` — THERE. That's the crash. Line 91 ≈ filter loop. OK so call 2's partial home on 5:2 has: header instance, hero complete, listings heading + seeAll link. Missing: filter bar (crashed mid-way — first filter instance created but label/placeholder unchanged, not yet appended... createInstance adds to page root! So a stray Field/Filter instance sits on 5:2 root), then grid etc.\n\nCleanup then rebuild plan:\n1. Delete stray 01_Home frame on page 0:1 (id starts 7: or 9:). Find by name among 0:1 children where id ≠ 4:14.\n2. On 5:2: delete partial '01_Home' and any stray instances at page root (Field/Filter orphan).\n3. Rebuild 01_Home fully with fixed findOne logic (use children indices for fields).\n4. Continue with testimonial, agency sections, CTA, footer.\n\nFor robustness, ALWAYS start each call with `await figma.setCurrentPageAsync(await figma.getNodeByIdAsync('5:2'))`.\n\nField text access pattern: \n```\nconst label = f.children[0]; // TEXT label\nconst input = f.children.find(c => c.type === 'FRAME'); // Input frame\nconst ph = input.children[0]; // placeholder text (before chevron instance)\n```\nIn Field/Filter the Input children: [TEXT placeholder, INSTANCE chevron]. Yes.\n\nLet me do cleanup + verify now.","signature":""}],"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8420bd2b-08e1-4650-9759-ee0744cf2f7f","uuid":"538abe54-962b-4348-9efb-277c430c2b7f","timestamp":"2026-11-01T23:58:54.262Z","thinking_duration_ms":105306}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"type":"message","model":"kimi-k3","usage":{"input_tokens":56568,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_5_m_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_1_h_tokens":0},"role":"assistant","id":"02036ae7d1ce0a7aed1f798128a91fbd","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"mcp__figma__use_figma_30_f2f6e5a3","name":"mcp__figma__use_figma","input":{"fileKey":"DevhQt2vYskNKsEjAoLgOj","code":"\nconst startPage = await figma.getNodeByIdAsync('0:1');\nconst ws = await figma.getNodeByIdAsync('5:2');\nconst removed = [];\n// 1) remove stray frames named 01_Home on Start Here page (not 4:14)\nfor (const c of [...startPage.children]) {\n  if (c.name === '01_Home' && c.id !== '4:14') { removed.push('0:1 stray ' + c.id); c.remove(); }\n}\n// 2) workspace: remove partial 01_Home and any stray instances at page root\nfor (const c of [...ws.children]) {\n  if (c.name === '01_Home' || (c.type === 'INSTANCE' && c.parent.type === 'PAGE')) {\n    removed.push('5:2 stray ' + c.id + ' ' + c.name); c.remove();\n  }\n}\nawait figma.setCurrentPageAsync(ws);\nreturn JSON.stringify({\n  removed,\n  startPageChildren: startPage.children.map(c => c.id + ':' + c.name.slice(0, 24)),\n  wsChildren: ws.children.map(c => c.id + ':' + c.name.slice(0, 24)),\n  currentPage: figma.currentPage.id,\n});\n","description":"Delete stray partial frames from both pages, reset current page to workspace"}}],"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8420bd2b-08e1-4650-9759-ee0744cf2f7f","uuid":"10aa7bef-ced2-41a5-a630-680d93b24b97","timestamp":"2026-11-01T23:58:58.367Z","tool_use_meta":[{"id":"mcp__figma__use_figma_30_f2f6e5a3","display_name":"Use Figma","server_display_name":"figma-authenticated-proxy","tool_name":"use_figma"}]}
